026-10-03T14:47:13.335Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
{"parentUuid":"2a284637-1fb4-42e8-b038-c81a99b9434b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b8557a2-9430-4f17-946a-1ea07dab5643","agentId":"afc43fc036aaadb5a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GB5X8eAdB3vWfhwiGXtoMB","type":"tool_result","content":"f860ff3 codex: bash-tool-result cell supported, recorded in runs/shell-exit-status (#91)\nbd7e1e2 rules: a recording outranks a doc; partial support is supported with deviations; unsupported may be shown by runs (#90)\ncfd2711 rules: judge per unit (subjects), module.yaml is the agent's, module-distinct (#89)\nd27fac9 capabilities: a false cell needs evidence; add pending for not-yet-mocked (#87)\n4f35b85 ci: pin sr-checks at sloprail main (Stop perf, RepoID, snapshot leaks: #179, #185) (#88)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"faf8c384-793a-4cec-875d-05da2cb9755d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:47:15.960Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2a284637-1fb4-42e8-b038-c81a99b9434b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
{"parentUuid":"8e62bb50-1046-41de-9bf0-0664d03501ae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b8557a2-9430-4f17-946a-1ea07dab5643","agentId":"afc43fc036aaadb5a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013hRiTUaMJPH2qxzfu7Ds53","type":"tool_result","content":"== https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks\n37:Installed plugins can also bundle lifecycle config through their plugin\n39:plugins](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins#bundled-mcp-servers-and-lifecycle-hooks) for the\n40:plugin packaging rules.\n54:Codex can also discover hooks bundled with enabled plugins. Plugin-bundled\n327:When managed policy and remote hooks are enabled, Work Cloud with local access and dots use admin-managed remote MCP hooks on the cloud orchestrator. Configure `mcp_tool` handlers in Global `requirements.toml`. Work Cloud without local access and personal accounts do not use these enterprise hooks. Command/shell, prompt, and agent handlers; hooks from local configuration, plugins, or local directories; environment-scoped hooks; and `SessionEnd` MCP hooks are not supported with cloud orchestration, even when tools execute locally. When both orchestration and execution are local, existing supported hooks continue to work in local-only Work and Codex threads. Admins can still configure supported managed hooks in Agent Security for those workflows.\n339:user, project, session, and plugin hooks while still allowing administrator\n372:  plugin sources, but still loads managed hooks from `requirements.toml` and\n375:## Plugin-bundled hooks\n377:When a plugin is enabled, Codex can load lifecycle hooks from that plugin\n380:By default, Codex looks for `hooks/hooks.json` inside the plugin root. A plugin\n382:`.codex-plugin/plugin.json`. The manifest entry can be a `./`-prefixed path, an\n393:Manifest hook paths are resolved relative to the plugin root and must stay\n397:Plugin hook commands receive these environment variables:\n399:- `PLUGIN_ROOT` is a Codex-specific extension that points to the installed\n400:  plugin root.\n401:- `PLUGIN_DATA` is a Codex-specific extension that points to the plugin's\n403:- Codex also sets `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` and `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA` for\n404:  compatibility with existing plugin hooks.\n406:Plugin hooks use the same event schema as other hooks. Installing or enabling a\n407:plugin doesn't automatically trust its hooks; Codex skips plugin-bundled hooks\n== https://cursor.com/docs/hooks\n3:Hooks let you observe, control, and extend the agent loop using custom scripts. Define hooks in `hooks.json` files at the project or user level, or install them through plugins from **Customize**. Hooks are spawned processes that communicate over stdio using JSON in both directions. They run before or after defined stages of the agent loop and can observe, block, or modify behavior.\n44:- `workspaceOpen` - Fires when Cursor opens a workspace and on every workspace folder change. Can return additional plugin paths to load for the current workspace.\n620:| [1Password](https://marketplace.1password.com/integration/cursor-hooks) | Validate that environment files from 1Password Environments are properly mounted before shell commands execute, enabling just-in-time secrets access without writing credentials to disk. |\n686:    \"workspaceOpen\": [{ \"command\": \"./register-workspace-plugins.sh\" }]\n1084:Match on `mcp_server_name` (and `tool_name`) to decide whether a call targets your server. `command` is the launch string from the server's config and can differ between installs: relative paths, `${CURSOR_PLUGIN_ROOT}` expansion, or an HTTP transport (which has no `command` at all). A hook that allows anything it does not recognize should treat a missing or unexpected `mcp_server_name` as a deny.\n1441:  \"pluginPaths\": [\"<absolute path>\", \"...\"]\n1447:| `pluginPaths` | string\\[] (optional) | Absolute paths to plugin directories to load for the current workspace. |\n== https://cursor.com/docs/plugins\n1:# Plugins\n3:Plugins package rules, skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, and hooks into distributable bundles.\n5:Cursor supports the [Agent Plugins](https://agent-plugins.org) open standard alongside its own plugin format. Install and manage them from the [Customize](https://cursor.com/docs/customize-cursor.md) page or browse official plugins in the [Cursor Marketplace](/marketplace). For community plugins and MCP servers, browse [cursor.directory](https://cursor.directory). You can also [build your own](https://cursor.com/docs/plugins.md#creating-plugins) to share with other developers.\n7:## What plugins contain\n9:A plugin can bundle any combination of these components:\n13:| **Rules**       | Cursor Plugins | Persistent AI guidance and coding standards (`.mdc` files) |\n15:| **Agents**      | Cursor Plugins | Custom agent configurations and prompts                    |\n16:| **Commands**    | Cursor Plugins | Agent-executable command files                             |\n18:| **Hooks**       | Cursor Plugins | Automation scripts triggered by events                     |\n20:## The Agent Plugins standard\n22:Plugins bundle reusable components an agent can use. [Agent Plugins](https://agent-plugins.org)\n25:supports Agent Plugins alongside Cursor Plugins.\n27:- **Agent Plugins**: spec-conformant plugins with a `plugin.json` manifest at the plugin root, packaging skills and MCP servers\n28:- **Cursor Plugins**: plugins with a `.cursor-plugin/plugin.json` manifest, which add rules, agents, commands, hooks, and [variables](https://cursor.com/docs/reference/plugins.md#variables)\n30:A plugin that follows the Agent Plugins specification loads in Cursor. Cursor does not expand the standard's `${PLUGIN_ROOT}` and `${PLUGIN_DATA}` variables in `mcp.json`; use `${CURSOR_PLUGIN_ROOT}` for the plugin root. See [MCP servers](https://cursor.com/docs/reference/plugins.md#mcp-servers) in the reference. Cursor Plugins continue to develop in parallel with the standard, so Cursor-specific components and marketplace features keep working as they do today.\n32:Learn more at [agent-plugins.org](https://agent-plugins.org) or read the [specification on GitHub](https://github.com/agentplugins/agent-plugins-spec).\n34:## Cursor Plugin canvases\n36:Plugins now ship with prebuilt **canvases**: shared setup templates your team can open and reuse.\n41:Open a canvas from an installed plugin in Customize to get a guided starting point instead of configuring everything from scratch.\n43:## The marketplace","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c257068d-12e8-41fc-9c96-bae5279a27e1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:47:20.565Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8e62bb50-1046-41de-9bf0-0664d03501ae","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
