{"parentUuid":"ec52e22e-91cb-44ab-b6b8-56a64d8d933e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afc43fc036aaadb5a","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CffVAcXTcNVfzViSoBxGr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XUroR8EfXvS4RHiQDzigLM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"curl -fsSL https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks.md | sed -n 30,60p; curl -fsSL https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks.md | sed -n 370,420p; curl -fsSL https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins.md | grep -n -i -B2 -A25 \"marketplace\" | head -150"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2941,"cache_read_input_tokens":70817,"output_tokens":224,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2941},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":224,"cache_read_input_tokens":70817,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2941,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2941,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XUroR8EfXvS4RHiQDzigLM":{"command":"cd /Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a && curl -fsSL https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks.md | sed -n 30,60p; curl -fsSL https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks.md | sed -n 370,420p; curl -fsSL https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins.md | grep -n -i -B2 -A25 \"marketplace\" | head -150"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01XUroR8EfXvS4RHiQDzigLM":{"cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffVAcFaeTBLjK9amYcTx","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"17b0b147-9c04-4761-8d73-ce1967286756","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:47:23.695Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
{"parentUuid":"17b0b147-9c04-4761-8d73-ce1967286756","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6b8557a2-9430-4f17-946a-1ea07dab5643","agentId":"afc43fc036aaadb5a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XUroR8EfXvS4RHiQDzigLM","type":"tool_result","content":"## Where Codex looks for hooks\n\nCodex discovers hooks next to active config layers in either of these forms:\n\n- `hooks.json`\n- inline `[hooks]` tables inside `config.toml`\n\nInstalled plugins can also bundle lifecycle config through their plugin\nmanifest or a default `hooks/hooks.json` file. See [Build\nplugins](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins#bundled-mcp-servers-and-lifecycle-hooks) for the\nplugin packaging rules.\n\nIn practice, the four most useful locations are:\n\n- `~/.codex/hooks.json`\n- `~/.codex/config.toml`\n- `<repo>/.codex/hooks.json`\n- `<repo>/.codex/config.toml`\n\nIf more than one hook source exists, Codex loads all matching hooks.\nHigher-precedence config layers don't replace lower-precedence hooks.\nIf a single layer contains both `hooks.json` and inline `[hooks]`, Codex\nmerges them and warns at startup. Prefer one representation per layer.\n\nCodex can also discover hooks bundled with enabled plugins. Plugin-bundled\nhooks load alongside other hook sources and use the same trust-review flow as\nother non-managed hooks.\n\nProject-local hooks load only when the project `.codex/` layer is trusted. In\nuntrusted projects, Codex still loads user and system hooks from their own\nactive config layers.\n  managed directory.\n- `allow_managed_hooks_only = true` skips hooks from user, project, session, and\n  plugin sources, but still loads managed hooks from `requirements.toml` and\n  other managed config layers.\n\n## Plugin-bundled hooks\n\nWhen a plugin is enabled, Codex can load lifecycle hooks from that plugin\nalongside user, project, and managed hooks.\n\nBy default, Codex looks for `hooks/hooks.json` inside the plugin root. A plugin\nmanifest can override that default with a `hooks` entry in\n`.codex-plugin/plugin.json`. The manifest entry can be a `./`-prefixed path, an\narray of `./`-prefixed paths, an inline hooks object, or an array of inline\nhooks objects.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"repo-policy\",\n  \"hooks\": \"./hooks/hooks.json\"\n}\n```\n\nManifest hook paths are resolved relative to the plugin root and must stay\ninside that root. If a manifest defines `hooks`, Codex uses those manifest\nentries instead of the default `hooks/hooks.json`.\n\nPlugin hook commands receive these environment variables:\n\n- `PLUGIN_ROOT` is a Codex-specific extension that points to the installed\n  plugin root.\n- `PLUGIN_DATA` is a Codex-specific extension that points to the plugin's\n  writable data directory.\n- Codex also sets `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` and `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA` for\n  compatibility with existing plugin hooks.\n\nPlugin hooks use the same event schema as other hooks. Installing or enabling a\nplugin doesn't automatically trust its hooks; Codex skips plugin-bundled hooks\nuntil you review and trust the current hook definition.\n\n## Matcher patterns\n\nThe `matcher` field is a regex string that filters when hooks fire. Use `\"*\"`,\n`\"\"`, or omit `matcher` entirely to match every occurrence of a supported\nevent.\n\nOnly some current Codex events honor `matcher`:\n\n| Event               | What `matcher` filters | Notes                                                        |\n| ------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `PermissionRequest` | tool name              | Support includes `Bash`, `apply_patch`\\*, and MCP tool names |\n29-\n30-Public plugins are published once to the universal plugin directory shared by\n31:ChatGPT and Codex. Local and repo marketplaces are separate authoring, testing,\n32-and team-distribution sources, and their availability can vary by surface.\n33-\n34-Use `@plugin-creator` for the fastest OpenAI-specific path, or create the\n35-portable manifest and folder structure manually.\n36-\n37-For complete public examples, inspect\n38-[Figma](https://github.com/openai/plugins/tree/main/plugins/figma),\n39-[Notion](https://github.com/openai/plugins/tree/main/plugins/notion), and\n40-[Build web apps](https://github.com/openai/plugins/tree/main/plugins/build-web-apps).\n41-\n42-## Package with `@plugin-creator`\n43-\n44-For the fastest setup, use the built-in `@plugin-creator` skill.\n45-\n46-\n47-\n48-![Plugin creator skill in ChatGPT](<https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/plugins/plugin-creator.png>)\n49-\n50-\n51-\n52-It scaffolds a supported `.codex-plugin/plugin.json` compatibility manifest\n53:and can also generate a local marketplace entry for testing. If you already\n54-have a plugin folder, you can still use `@plugin-creator` to wire it into a\n55:local marketplace.\n56-\n57-### Plugin creator output\n58-\n59-The current scaffold uses the Codex compatibility layout, not the portable\n60-Agent Plugins layout. When you request all optional components, it can create:\n61-\n62-```text\n63-my-plugin/\n64-├── .codex-plugin/\n65-│   └── plugin.json\n66-├── .mcp.json\n67-├── .app.json\n68-├── skills/\n69-├── hooks/\n70-├── scripts/\n71-└── assets/\n72-```\n73-\n74-Only `.codex-plugin/plugin.json` is always created. The other files and\n75-directories are optional. `.mcp.json` starts with an empty `mcpServers` object,\n76-and `.app.json` starts with an empty `apps` object. The manifest references\n77-these files when requested. It also declares `skills: \"./skills/\"`; add your\n78-skill folders there before testing a skills-based plugin.\n79-\n80-Requesting hooks creates an empty `hooks/` directory, not a hook configuration\n--\n132-    `{`@plugin-creator create a plugin for ChatGPT and Codex using my MCP server.\n133-Use plugin_asdk_app_6a4c0062f3b88191855c0a80eac5d53d and name it Acme Support.\n134:Include a personal marketplace entry so I can test it locally.`}`\n135-  \n136-\n137-\n138-\n139-\n140-The plugin-creator skill will create the plugin folder, create a supported\n141-`.codex-plugin/plugin.json` compatibility manifest, and add MCP server wiring\n142:for the plugin. If you ask it to create a personal marketplace entry, the\n143-plugin appears under your local source in the Plugins Directory for testing.\n144-\n145-After the plugin-creator skill creates the plugin:\n146-\n147-1. Review `.app.json` and confirm the registered MCP server mapping points at\n148-   the correct `plugin_asdk_app...` ID.\n149-2. Review `.codex-plugin/plugin.json` and make sure its `apps`\n150-   field points to `./.app.json`.\n151-3. Add any bundled skills under `skills/` if the plugin should include\n152-   repeatable workflows alongside the MCP server.\n153:4. If the skill created a personal marketplace entry, refresh ChatGPT\n154-   and install the plugin from your local source in the Plugins Directory. Then\n155-   test it in a new chat.\n156-\n157-For the manifest shape and file layout, see [Plugin structure](#plugin-structure)\n158-and [Path rules](#path-rules).\n159-\n160-### Build your own curated plugin list\n161-\n162:A marketplace is a JSON catalog of plugins. `@plugin-creator` can generate one\n163:for a single plugin, and you can keep adding entries to that same marketplace\n164-to build your own curated list for a repo, team, or personal workflow.\n165-\n166:In Work mode or Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app, each marketplace appears as a\n167-selectable source in the Plugins Directory. Use\n168:`$REPO_ROOT/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json` for a repo-scoped list or\n169:`~/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json` for a personal list. Add one entry per\n170-plugin under `plugins[]`, point each `source.path` at the plugin folder with a\n171:`./`-prefixed path relative to the marketplace root, and set\n172:`interface.displayName` to the label you want the plugin to show in the marketplace\n173-picker. Then restart the ChatGPT desktop app. After that, open the Plugins\n174:Directory, choose your marketplace, and browse or install the plugins in that\n175-curated list.\n176-\n177:You don't need a separate marketplace per plugin. One marketplace can expose a\n178-single plugin while you are testing, then grow into a larger curated catalog as\n179-you add more plugins.\n180-\n181-\n182-\n183:![custom local marketplace in the Plugins Directory](<https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/plugins/codex-local-plugin-light.png>)\n184-\n185-\n186-\n187:### Add a marketplace from the CLI\n188-\n189:Use `codex plugin marketplace add` to add and track a marketplace source instead\n190-of editing `config.toml` by hand. These commands support plugin authoring and\n191-catalog setup. Use the ChatGPT desktop app to install and test a local plugin.\n192-\n193-```bash\n194:codex plugin marketplace add owner/repo\n195:codex plugin marketplace add owner/repo --ref main\n196:codex plugin marketplace add https://github.com/example/plugins.git --sparse .agents/plugins\n197:codex plugin marketplace add ./local-marketplace-root\n198-```\n199-\n200:Marketplace sources can be GitHub shorthand (`owner/repo` or\n201:`owner/repo@ref`), HTTP or HTTPS Git URLs, SSH Git URLs, or local marketplace root\n202-directories. Use `--ref` to pin a Git ref, and repeat `--sparse PATH` to use a\n203:sparse checkout for Git-backed marketplace repos. `--sparse` is valid only for\n204:Git marketplace sources.\n205-\n206:To inspect, refresh, or remove configured marketplaces:\n207-\n208-```bash\n209:codex plugin marketplace list\n210:codex plugin marketplace upgrade\n211:codex plugin marketplace upgrade marketplace-name\n212:codex plugin marketplace remove marketplace-name\n213-```\n214-\n215:`codex plugin marketplace list` prints each marketplace Codex is considering\n216:and the root path it resolves from, including local default marketplaces and\n217:configured marketplace snapshots.\n218-\n219:Administrators can also define local or Git marketplaces in system\n220-`config.toml` or cloud-managed configuration. These sources use the same\n221:marketplace catalog format. See [Configure plugin marketplaces and\n222:defaults](https://developers.openai.com/codex/enterprise/managed-configuration#configure-plugin-marketplaces-and-defaults)\n223-for managed setup guidance and links to the configuration reference.\n224-\n225-### Create a plugin manually\n226-\n227-Start with a minimal plugin that packages one skill.\n228-","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d8172dc0-547f-4a77-966f-4817d0d622c2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:47:26.623Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"17b0b147-9c04-4761-8d73-ce1967286756","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-afc43fc036aaadb5a","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"claude/capabilities-matrix-impl-2a0cfd"}
