{"parentUuid":"dbb6e125-05b5-4dd6-8f25-7a748b91aa82","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afc43fc036aaadb5a","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZNb3wzqXKTSxtPPUiH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The rebase onto `origin/main` hit one conflict, in `codex-mock/e2e/001_hooks/helpers_test.go`. I kept both sides (the new `ProjectHooksJSON` field and my `HomeFiles` field) and finished the rebase cleanly. The branch now has two commits: the main work and a new cursor unit test. I haven't re-run the cursor tests since the rebase, so I'm not claiming they pass. The background job builds, runs the plugin-hooks tests for both mocks, then starts `sr-checks`. It's still running.\n\nThe earlier `sr-checks` run refused on one point about my work. It said cursor's default `hooks/hooks.json` discovery was never tested, because my recording names the hooks file in the plugin manifest. The new unit test, `cursor-mock/internal/hooks/plugin_test.go`, covers it: a plugin with no hooks entry in its manifest, one that names a file, and one that isn't loaded. It passes.\n\nThat run also refused about 15 other points, about other capabilities (for example `subprocess-session-env/codex`, `user-prompt-submit-hook/codex` and `session-start-hook/cursor`). Those capabilities were pulled into the judging because my changeset touches `capture.sh` and the MANIFEST files. I'm not rewriting them. If the new run shows the same ones, I'll report them as pre-existing.\n\nNothing is pushed, no PR is open and nothing is merged yet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":658,"cache_read_input_tokens":210777,"output_tokens":487,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":658},"inference_ge